\f1\b\fs22 The spine: an island of irony above a sea of faith\

\f2\b0 Look at who's on that VIP platform and you'll notice they have one thing in common \'97 not one of them actually believes the Evolution-Elvi religion. Sarah is the worshipped idol who feels everything and shows nothing. Chanta is about to write the anthem 
\f3\i against
\f2\i0  the religion. Eloise believes in no cause but the profit from the threat of one. Kathy and Hayley are your clear-eyed outsiders (the notes already frame Hayley as the one who reads the trap in the triumph). The only true believers in the whole half-kilometre paddock are the half-million below \'97 and the three Sages, who believe fervently, but in something else entirely. So the platform in front of the engineering tower isn't just staging; it's the scene's architecture. It's the one place the meme doesn't reach, an island of the un-converted floating above a field being converted in real time. Every character bump you want to stage gets sharper if you play it as 
\f3\i these particular non-believers, cornered together, watching belief happen to everyone else.
\f2\i0  That's the frame I'd hang the whole scene on.\
The "under Airborne noses" question resolves inside that same frame, and I think you're right that the prosaic answer is the strong one \'97 but it needs one turn to reconcile with your own notes, because the notes want the concert to be a provocation that forces Dunhilton to bring the virus forward, and "nobody noticed, it's just another Domain event" pulls the opposite way. The reconciliation is a seam, not a contradiction: 
\f1\b it passes unremarked 
\f4\i on the day
\f1\i0  and detonates 
\f4\i on the broadcast.
\f2\i0\b0  The Domain hosts events constantly; the permits are "mysteriously approved" (Eloise's invisible hand \'97 already in your synopsis), so officially it's just another sanctioned booking, and the Airborne who wander in treat the whole thing \'97 the puking, the punch-ups, the giant glowing balloon \'97 as a deliciously ironic slumming-it day out. They think they're spectating. They don't clock that they're the most valuable subjects in the experiment. What turns it into a provocation isn't the lawn; it's the live feed and "Religious Minds" going to number one worldwide (your notes: Dunhilton grinds his teeth to three emergency dental visits). So the Airborne nose-blindness and the government panic aren't in tension \'97 the first is the daytime, the second is the aftermath. And the joke lands harder for it: the meritocracy let this onto its own grass because it couldn't see a threat that didn't look like one.\

\f1\b The Sages \'97 your big gap\

\f2\b0 Here's the thing that closes the gap: a mass event whose entire purpose is to install one belief in half a million people by song, replacing an old belief (gospel) with a new one (Dawkins), is not a scene the Sages 
\f3\i attend
\f2\i0 . It's a scene they've been waiting centuries for. You've already drafted the small-scale version of exactly this \'97 "The intervention," where Kleo proves that "Praise the Dawkins" breaks the gospel spell on Dr Carpenter, and Kathy realises they're not curing the contagion, they're 
\f3\i learning the operating system of belief
\f2\i0 : "if you can swap gospel for Dawkins, you can swap Dawkins for anything." Rock Against Revival is that same experiment run at scale. Their end isn't the concert's success or failure \'97 it's the 
\f3\i proof
\f2\i0  that belief is content-independent and installable at will, because that's what the prophecy needs: a world that can be made to believe in Little Clinton as "the One After the One" on command. So the Sages aren't steering toward evolution winning. They're steering toward 
\f3\i unison
\f2\i0  \'97 one crowd, one song, one belief \'97 because unison is the thing they need to prove they can manufacture. The content is beneath their notice.\
That gives each of them a role that's just their canon turned up:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Chilo
\f2\b0 \'a0stokes the chaos. He's the schemer who runs his own agenda outside the trio ("the others don't know I'm here"), the one who blurts the apocalyptic quiet part and drives escalation. He's the hand behind the lyric-fights \'97 nudging the early bands to sing the wrong words, letting the punch-ups build, because friction is data: he's measuring exactly how a crowd converges on one meme when you let it fight first. And he's the one running the dangerous private experiment during the climax (see below).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Solo
\f2\b0 \'a0works the green room and the crowd \'97 the affable front-man, "always the one to rescue tragic scenes," distracting the marks, keeping morale up, genuinely fond of everyone he's manipulating. He's also the conscience who gets uneasy ("This isn't what we discussed," Solo hissed) when Chilo goes too far. He's where you put the flicker of\'a0
\f3\i these beings are not entirely comfortable with what they're doing.
\f2\i0 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Kleo
\f2\b0 \'a0observes and, at most, plants one compulsion. Remember what he did to Agent Jones \'97 "You want to worship her," and Jones sagged. The chilling version is that Kleo\'a0
\f3\i doesn't need to
\f2\i0 \'a0do that here. He watches half a million people install a belief with no help from him and simply notes it. His weary line to Solo could be the scene's thesis stated flat:\'a0
\f3\i we used to have to whisper. Now they do it themselves.
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 What are they physically doing during "Religious Minds"? I'd put all three on or beneath the engineering tower \'97 which makes the tower the panopticon and collides them with Chanta, who's up there for his vision. Kleo watches. Solo frets. And 
\f1\b Chilo runs a Gyges-disc experiment
\f2\b0  on the crowd's unison \'97 treating half a million voices locked on one refrain as a resonance he can read, or feed. This is where you seed Part 6: recall the classified rule that 
\f3\i stasis fields must never be allowed to resonate with one another
\f2\i0 , and Chilo's whole "accelerating inevitability" agenda to get the child born "so a new perfect universe can bang into place." Mass belief-unison as a kind of resonance the disc responds to is a lovely, sinister invention that costs you nothing now and pays off later. Solo notices and hisses. Kleo either doesn't see it or pretends not to. The reader files it away.\
And here's the payoff that makes the Sages 
\f3\i earn
\f2\i0  their place rather than just decorate it \'97 it fuses them to the ending irony you already love. Chanta writes "Religious Minds" to attack 
\f3\i all
\f2\i0  religion, the Holy Rollers and the Dawkins-worship alike. But the Sages have proven belief is content-independent \'97 so an anti-religion anthem, sung in ecstatic worshipful unison by half a million people in adoration of the Great Teacher who wrote it and the Holy Momma beside him, doesn't 
\f3\i puncture
\f2\i0  the religion. It 
\f3\i becomes
\f2\i0  the new liturgy. The crowd sings "we can't go on with religious minds" as the single most religious act of the day. Your existing closing beat \'97 the High Elvis chants "Praise the Dawkins" and "no one recognised the irony" \'97 stops being a throwaway gag and becomes the Sages' thesis validated in front of us. The one who recognises the irony is Kleo, from the foot of the tower, and he's satisfied.\

\f1\b Eloise, and how she gets onto that platform\

\f2\b0 Your instinct is exactly right and it's pure Eloise: she'd never let her loaned hardware be handed to the Elvi for concert security and left to run unsupervised. She has firmware backdoors on all of it (canon \'97 she shut down the whole theatre of PAPER and Elvi hardware to rescue Sarah), so "supervising the loan" is the pretext, but the real reason she comes is Sarah: her succession investment, carrying the baby she's staked her empire on, has slipped out to a rock concert in the open. So Eloise arrives 
\f3\i mid-event
\f2\i0 , not at the start, and the beat you want writes itself as a visual. The Elvi have built two burlesque thrones for the Holy Momma and the Great Teacher and two seats for the Aunts \'97 sacred mythological furniture. Eloise has her own chair 
\f3\i brought
\f2\i0  and set down next to Sarah: something corporate and ergonomic and utterly un-throne-like, an expensive office chair amid the melted-bobblehead crowns. It's a statement of ownership \'97 
\f3\i this one is mine, not your mythology's
\f2\i0  \'97 and it puts the arms-dealer atheist physically between Sarah and the Elvi's religion. She watches the crowd get converted the way she reads a weapons market: not with alarm, with appraisal. "I never seek war, merely the profit from the threat of it." She, Kathy, and Hayley (already her recruits \'97 "bright, loyal, brutal") become the clear-eyed corner of the platform, and the look that passes between Kathy and Hayley across the chaos (your notes) happens over Eloise's shoulder while she's pricing the revolution.\
One flag: in the Inauguration scene, "Eloise didn't know she was here" \'97 Sarah sneaks out. Here Sarah 
\f3\i sneaks out again
\f2\i0  (and Eloise tracks her and follows, which fits the surveillance-mode Eloise of "Kathy fetches the Sages"). This gives Eloise a reason to sweep in with a face like weather.\

\f1\b The floating icon \'97 your Elvis "pig"\

\f2\b0 Don't reach for a giant inflatable Elvis; it's the obvious note and it does no narrative work. I'd tether, above the engineering tower, 
\f1\b a vast, softly glowing inflatable foetus \'97 Little Clinton, "the One After the One," as a Macy's-parade balloon.
\f2\b0  It does three jobs at once. It's the concert's central idol, which means the Evolution Elvi have literally built and worship a sacred balloon of an unborn baby \'97 Exhibit A for Chanta's realisation that they've made a religion every bit as credulous as Humble's. It ties straight to Chanta's own recurring vision of Little Clinton as "an orange ball of light in Sarah's belly," so when he goes up the tower and trips, he's flying toward the very thing he keeps hallucinating, now forty feet tall and lit from within. And it gives you a gorgeous crowd image for the "Most Have Never Stirred" ballad: half a million people holding up orange glow-orbs (sold at the merch tents \'97 see below), a field of little bellies-of-light under one giant one. Pink Floyd floated a pig over a power station to say 
\f3\i this is what capital looks like
\f2\i0 ; you float a luminous unborn god over a crowd to say 
\f3\i this is what they've decided to worship,
\f2\i0  and your protagonist writes the anthem against it while staring up at it.\
If you want a runner-up with a different flavour, the 
\f1\b empty ascended jumpsuit
\f2\b0  \'97 a white rhinestone suit, arms outstretched, floating with no one in it \'97 is thematically rich for a book about clones of a dead messiah (the King ascended; the suit is the absence where the god should be). But it competes with the foetus for the same sky and I think the foetus wins because it integrates with Chanta's vision and the prophecy. Keep the jumpsuit in your pocket. And for tonal relief you can always fly a smaller peanut-butter-banana-and-bacon-sandwich blimp somewhere in the frame; someone will have paid a fortune for the ad space.\

\f1\b Merch and bar \'97 texture, with two items that pull double duty\

\f2\b0 Your list is good; here's more, and I've starred the two that do narrative work rather than just colour:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f5 \uc0\u11088 
\f2 \'a0
\f1\b Orange glow-orbs
\f2\b0 \'a0\'97 "hold up the One After the One." These are the belly-glow motif sold back to the crowd; they're what makes the ballad's sea-of-light image, and they're what Chanta sees from the tower. This is the merch item that matters.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f5 \uc0\u11088 
\f2 \'a0
\f1\b Air guitars, upsold to absurdity
\f2\b0 \'a0\'97 you've already got the joke (a lot of money for nothing); push it into satire of the Airborne specifically. "Air Guitar Pro" in a velvet case containing certified nothing; "artisanal air" at a premium; a limited-edition queue. The gag is that the Cloudborne buy sideburns and the Airborne pay triple for\'a0
\f3\i nothing at all
\f2\i0 \'a0and feel they've got the better deal \'97 the whole class-satire of the book in a single stall.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Sacred sunglasses \'97 knockoff "Great Teacher" shades (his are "sacred now," per the Vegas-platform scene); everyone wants the holy eyewear.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}"Praise the Dawkins" foam fingers and glow sticks; TCB lightning-bolt pendants (a real Elvis motif, so free comedy for readers who know it).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Adult-sized "One After the One" onesies; glow-in-the-dark foetus pendants (the idol, wearable).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Peel-and-stick rhinestones \'97 bedazzle-your-own; edible liquorice sideburns; peanut-butter-banana scent cards; fried-sandwich-scented votive candles.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Capes printed with your own Layer \'97 Airborne buying Cloudborne cosplay, and vice versa, the class line dissolving through the gift shop.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 On the bar: the detail that sells it is that the alcohol is nearly nonexistent \'97 it's the 
\f3\i sugar and fat
\f2\i0  that fells the Airborne, whose bodies have never processed an Elvi cocktail (peanut-butter-and-bacon everything). So the puking around the fringes as the afternoon heat climbs isn't drunkenness, it's the Airborne physically unable to stomach Cloudborne pleasure \'97 a small, funny, precise class-body joke rather than generic festival grot. Keep it exactly that specific.\

\f1\b Bands, punch-ups, and the High Elvis's three laws\

\f2\b0 The escalation structure is strong \'97 three-pieces growing to a full Vegas big band with brass and strings as the sun goes down, a queue of Elvi side-stage each fronting a number (you've got this exact image in the Vegas scene, so it'll rhyme). The reason the early bands sing non-Great-Teacher lyrics and get stormed is canon and you should lean on it: "we can all agree on the music, but never the lyrics \'97 it's like God" (Lonnie, Vegas scene). That's the whole engine. And it's what makes the punch-ups 
\f3\i the Sages' experiment
\f2\i0  rather than random slapstick \'97 Chilo is down there letting the lyric-wars run because he's watching a crowd fail to converge, then converge.\
the High Elvis's intervention should be played dead calm \'97 your notes insist he's "calm and counselling, never bombast," which is what makes the comic triplet land. He gravely lays down the law:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 {\listtext	1	}Sing the words of the Great Teacher.\
{\listtext	2	}Do not punch up on anyone who does not sing the words of the Great Teacher.\
{\listtext	3	}Sing the words of the Great Teacher.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Law 2 is the one nobody can keep \'97 it's the only one asking them 
\f3\i not
\f2\i0  to do the thing they love \'97 and the joke is that he has to bookend it with the identical first and third commandment to smuggle it through, and it 
\f3\i still
\f2\i0  doesn't take. There's a quiet structural irony worth catching: the High Elvis wants 
\f3\i order
\f2\i0  (less fighting, more unison), the Sages want 
\f3\i unison too
\f2\i0  \'97 but for opposite reasons, and the Sages are indifferent to the violence the High Elvis is trying to stop. So his three laws are, without his knowing it, doing the Sages' work: driving the crowd toward one set of words. He thinks he's keeping the peace; he's installing the meme.\

\f1\b Chanta's out-of-body genesis \'97 and one continuity flag you should decide on\

\f2\b0 The Tom Robbins sequence is the best thing in your brain-dump and I'd protect it hard: despondent all day over Sarah and Little Clinton, avoiding his own throne, carousing then climbing the engineering tower to be nearer the baby "for inspiration"; the setting sun becoming the orange ball of light in Sarah's belly; the psychotropics and the cocktail-sugar tipping him out of his body; the flight down to occupy the throne beside Sarah; and from 
\f3\i her
\f2\i0  vantage, looking out at the crowd, the realisation \'97 
\f3\i they're not celebrating, they're worshipping; the Evolution Elvi have made Sarah and the baby into gods; they're as religious as the Holy Rollers, and I have to write against all of it.
\f2\i0  The lyrics come too fast to hold; he grabs a notepad from beside the sound desk and scribbles; texts the High Elvis 
\f3\i I have a song
\f2\i0 ; rushes back. Keep all of it.\
But here's the flag, and it's a real one. Your own decisions-log (captured in Chanta's file) is emphatic that the "Religious Minds" block-break is 
\f1\b decoupled from Sarah
\f2\b0  \'97 the whole point of the arc is that Chanta 
\f3\i believes
\f2\i0  the baby is his muse and the story keeps 
\f3\i disproving
\f2\i0  it (he wrote "Let Your Momma Go" after she left; he creates without her). If you stage the song's birth as 
\f3\i he flies to be near Sarah and the baby and therefore the song comes
\f2\i0 , you risk re-confirming the muse theory the author has spent Part 5 dismantling. The fix is subtle and I think it makes the scene better: keep the vision, but make the 
\f3\i trigger
\f2\i0  rage, not proximity. He climbs the tower 
\f3\i thinking
\f2\i0  he needs the baby's nearness \'97 that's his muse theory, still wrong. What actually breaks the block is fury: the Holy Rollers wrecked the Memphis Mafia Bar and are gutting the Ghetto, and now, from the god's-eye view, he sees his 
\f3\i own
\f2\i0  side has built a giant glowing idol of the baby and is worshipping it. The song comes from that anger, exactly as your log says ("grief and rage over the Bar, not Sarah's return"). Chanta will 
\f3\i attribute
\f2\i0  it to Little Clinton \'97 he always does \'97 but we the readers watch it come from rage. The muse theory stays disproved; the irony stays intact; and you keep every image in the sequence. Decide it consciously, because the na\'efve staging quietly undoes a thread you were careful about.\
(Two smaller reads to lock: I'd keep the flight to the throne purely visionary \'97 his body stays slumped on the tower scribbling while his spirit sits beside Sarah \'97 so you preserve the notes' "Chanta never goes on." And the God's-eye POV isn't decoration; it's the 
\f3\i device
\f2\i0  that delivers the realisation, because you can only see a crowd worshipping when you're above it looking down with them as the altar.)\

\f1\b The "Religious Minds" set piece \'97 a model for the interleave, plus a rights flag you'll want\

\f2\b0 You already have the full lyric in the Unused Lyrics folder, so the work here is intercutting it against reactions. The principle: the lyric is the through-line, and you cut away on the 
\f3\i turns
\f2\i0  \'97 the lines that mean something different to each watcher. Sarah hears an accusation she can't answer; Eloise hears a product; the Sages hear proof; Humble, in the Ghetto, hears his own flock defect. Here's a model of the first verse-and-chorus to show the technique \'97 draft, in your register, for you to react to:\
the High Elvis lets the band fall to a heartbeat of bass. "The Great Teacher has written something new," he says, calm as a man reading a weather report. "Never played. Here it is."\

\f3\i You're caught in a church, you can't walk out \'97
\f2\i0 \
Half a million people who have never heard the song know, by the second line, that it is theirs. On the tower, Chilo stops pretending to watch the lighting rig.\

\f3\i Because you're praying too much, baby \'97
\f2\i0 \
Sarah feels the baby turn. She keeps her face the way Eloise taught her to keep it, but her hand has gone to her belly and she makes it come back. Beside her, in the wrong chair, Eloise watches the crowd and not the stage. "Number one by Tuesday," she says, to no one. "Every market. Tax it."\

\f3\i We can't go on as Elvi \'97 with religious minds \'97
\f2\i0 \
And the field sings the refrain back before the High Elvis reaches it, a hundred thousand voices arriving at the same word at the same instant, and Kleo, at the foot of the tower, closes his eyes the way a man does when a machine he built turns over first try. "We used to have to whisper," he says. Solo doesn't answer. Solo is watching Chilo's hands.\
In the Ghetto, in a walk-in cooler that used to be a bar, Pastor Rex Humble hears it come through the floor, and three of his own are already mouthing the words.\
That's the shape \'97 lyric, cut, lyric, cut, each cut a different pair of ears \'97 and you can run it clear through the song, giving Kathy and Hayley their look, Dunhilton his choke on the artisanal water, and Chilo's disc its quiet moment. I'll draft the whole thing if you want it; this is just the method.\
The flag I owe you, because this whole session started on it: 
\f1\b "Religious Minds" is the same exposure as "Most Have Never Stirred," and arguably a hair worse.
\f2\b0  It's a rewrite of "Suspicious Minds" (Mark James, 1968) \'97 a living-estate copyright \'97 and it tracks the original's structure and phrasing closely ("we can't go on together / with suspicious minds" 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  "we can't go on as Elvi / with religious minds," "caught in a trap" 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  "caught in a church," "we can't build our dreams" 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  "we can't build our freedom"). The wrinkle is the parody/satire line that decides fair use. The Supreme Court protected 2 Live Crew's parody of Roy Orbison's "Oh, Pretty Woman" precisely because it 
\f3\i commented on the original song
\f2\i0 . "Religious Minds" doesn't comment on "Suspicious Minds" \'97 it borrows its recognisable form to say something about religion. That's satire riding a copyrighted vehicle, which is the weaker footing, same as the Dawkins piece. And it's not one song: the High Elvis's inauguration speech is a mosaic of a dozen Elvis titles, and the entire Elvi conceit runs on mutated Elvis lyrics \'97 so this is a 
\f3\i book-level
\f2\i0  rights question (song copyrights 
\f3\i and
\f2\i0  the Presley estate's publicity rights over the name and persona), not something to solve one lyric at a time. I'm not saying don't do it \'97 the whole novel depends on it and there are real arguments \'97 I'm saying file it as a known, structural clearance problem for the publisher's rights team, exactly as we said for the Dawkins passage, and don't let the number-one-worldwide fantasy in your scene notes lull you into thinking the song is unproblematically yours.\
\
That's the full pass. Two open questions I couldn't resolve from the artefacts, both of which change what the scene 
\f3\i sets up
\f2\i0 : does Rock Against Revival feed 
\f3\i into
\f2\i0  "The Humpening" (your planned close-of-Part-5 orgy-festival that spreads the immunity virus \'97 the gospel-contagion tracker treats them as adjacent beats), or is it a clean, separate event? And do you want me to go deeper on any one section \'97 most usefully, draft the complete interleaved "Religious Minds" performance, or write up the Sages' through-line as something I can drop into the scene's notes once you've reacted? I haven't committed anything to the artefacts; say the word on which pieces you want kept and I'll fold them into the brief.\

